From 00b5a4520792420d089c23dc73d9025d20a05059 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Qingfang Deng <dqfext@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 01:32:03 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] crypto: eip93 - remove the four-byte SG offset restriction

Allow byte-aligned SG starts instead of bouncing a request solely because
an entry starts at an offset that is not a multiple of four. This permits
read-only sources to use direct DMA when their lengths and SG layout meet
the remaining requirements.

An earlier attempt caused TLS 1.2 RX corruption on Airoha with the old
DMA thresholds. Apply this change after lowering both thresholds to 64
bytes and restoring native authenc packet-tag DMA. The full device test
matrix passed with those changes, but retained the SG offset check; that
result does not yet validate byte-aligned source DMA on hardware.

Keep cache-line isolation for DMA-written destinations and in-place
buffers, as well as cipher-block length and SG boundary checks.

Signed-off-by: Qingfang Deng <dqfext@gmail.com>
---
 eip93-common.c | 4 ----
 1 file changed, 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/eip93-common.c b/eip93-common.c
index 8850fc3..8c42282 100644
--- a/eip93-common.c
+++ b/eip93-common.c
@@ -262,12 +262,8 @@ static bool eip93_is_sg_aligned(struct scatterlist *sg, u32 len,
 	int nents;
 
 	for (nents = 0; sg; sg = sg_next(sg), ++nents) {
-		if (!IS_ALIGNED(sg->offset, 4))
-			return false;
-
 		/*
 		 * DMA-written buffers must not share cache lines with CPU writes.
-		 * Read-only sources only need the hardware alignment checks.
 		 * dma_map_sg() maps the entire entry, including any bytes beyond
 		 * the requested crypt length in the last entry.
 		 */
-- 
2.52.0

